@meta
id: 13-16
title: Brackets that group
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-groups, regex-verymagic
par: 54
minutes: 7
vscode: rename symbol twice, and it would also rename getPaymentById
open: lookup.provider.ts

@brief
The `ById` suffix is coming off two of the lookup methods. `getOrderById`
becomes `getOrder` and `getCustomerById` becomes `getCustomer` — the
definitions and every call, six places altogether. `getPaymentById`
keeps its name; the payments team own that one.

You could write `getOrderById|getCustomerById`. It works, and it repeats
`get` and `ById` for no reason. The alternation only needs to cover the
middle. Round brackets fence off the part of a pattern an operator
applies to, the same as they do in arithmetic.

The other half of the job is landing. A search lands at the start of
its match, on the `g`, and you want to delete four characters at the
*end* of it. Chapter 12's offsets can put you there.

@keys
| `(...)` | under `\v`, a group — alternation and quantifiers apply to the whole of it |
| `\(...\)` | the same group in the default dialect |
| `\vget(Order\|Customer)ById` | the suffix, on either of two names |
| `/pattern/e-3` | land three characters before the end of the match |
| `de` | delete to the end of the word |

@hints
Say `get`, then either name, then `ById`. The "either" needs brackets
so it does not swallow the `get` and the `ById`.
--
A search offset of `e-3` lands on the fourth character from the end of
the match — the `B` of `ById`. From there `de` deletes to the end of
the word, and `n` keeps the offset.
--
`/\vget(Order|Customer)ById/e-3` `Enter`, `de`, then `n.` five times.

@solution
/\vget(Order|Customer)ById/e-3<Enter>  de  n.  n.  n.  n.  n.  :wq<Enter>

Forty-seven keystrokes for six renames, and `getPaymentById` was never
in danger.

A group does two jobs. The first is scoping: `get(Order|Customer)ById`
means what you would expect, while `getOrder|CustomerById` means
something else entirely. The second is repetition: a quantifier after a
group repeats the whole group, so `(\d+\.){2}\d+` is "a number and a
dot, twice, then a number" — a three-part version in eleven characters.
`(ab)+` is one or more `ab`s, where `ab+` is an `a` and then one or
more `b`s.

The dialect difference is the usual one. Under `\v` the brackets are
bare and a literal bracket is `\(`; in the default dialect a group is
`\(...\)` and a literal bracket is bare, which is why `/)$` worked
without an escape in the drill.

The `/e-3` is worth a second look. The match is `getOrderById` or
`getCustomerById` — two different lengths — and yet `e-3` lands on the
`B` in both, because it counts from the end of whatever matched. An
offset from the *start* could not have done that. Landing where the
edit is, rather than where the match starts, is the theme of the last
part of this chapter, and vim has a far cleaner way to say it than
counting backwards.

@start lookup.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { PaymentRepository } from '../payment/payment.repository';

@injectable()
export class LookupProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(PaymentRepository) protected paymentRepository: PaymentRepository,
  ) {}

  public async getOrderById(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async getCustomerById(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }

  public async getPaymentById(id: string) {
    const payment = await this.paymentRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!payment) {
      throw exception('PAYMENT_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return payment;
  }

  public async orderSummary(orderId: string) {
    const order = await this.getOrderById(orderId);
    const customer = await this.getCustomerById(order.customerId);
    const payment = await this.getPaymentById(order.paymentId);

    return { order, customer, payment };
  }

  public async customerOrders(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.getCustomerById(customerId);

    return this.orderRepository.findMany({ where: { customerId: customer.id } });
  }

  public async paymentOrder(paymentId: string) {
    const payment = await this.getPaymentById(paymentId);

    return this.getOrderById(payment.orderId);
  }
}

@expected lookup.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { PaymentRepository } from '../payment/payment.repository';

@injectable()
export class LookupProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(PaymentRepository) protected paymentRepository: PaymentRepository,
  ) {}

  public async getOrder(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async getCustomer(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }

  public async getPaymentById(id: string) {
    const payment = await this.paymentRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!payment) {
      throw exception('PAYMENT_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return payment;
  }

  public async orderSummary(orderId: string) {
    const order = await this.getOrder(orderId);
    const customer = await this.getCustomer(order.customerId);
    const payment = await this.getPaymentById(order.paymentId);

    return { order, customer, payment };
  }

  public async customerOrders(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.getCustomer(customerId);

    return this.orderRepository.findMany({ where: { customerId: customer.id } });
  }

  public async paymentOrder(paymentId: string) {
    const payment = await this.getPaymentById(paymentId);

    return this.getOrder(payment.orderId);
  }
}
